\ Dynamic compiler design ====================================================
\ Inlining is the big trick. Things need to be delayed and registers allocated
\ intelligently for good performance to be achieved.

\ Inlining applies to whole nonprimitives as well as to individual fast words.
\ It needs a way to see that whole phrases like   >R foo bar R>   can be
\ simplified by using a register to hold TOS for a bit.

\ XXX: Friction with the existing metacompiler: It expects compilation to be
\ pretty direct, streaming right into the target with an already-known xt.
\ We can still buffer up compilation and be smart about it, but it needs to
\ write the final form right away so that the xt of the next word can be known
\ ahead of time as well...
\ Then again, the main use of mirror>txt is while compiling calls to that word
\ in the code of other nonprimitives! So txt could be a lie, and it could
\ really be a host pointer or maybe an index into a host table for the AST of
\ the word being called, and we can do whole-program optimization and write it
\ all out.
\ That's probably ideal if I can pull it off!

\ In any case, the objective for now is to get this working for the entire
\ shared/* codebase so I can compile a complete app on DCPU and write a basic
\ game with it. Once that's working (even slowly) I can consider how to
\ optimize it and lower the friction inside the metacompiler, before porting to
\ GBA outright.
